{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run/draft && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nsrcs=[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S42-delay-propose-chan\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"replica/defs/latency.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":87,\\\"end_line\\\":135,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"S43-client-seqnum\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"client/client.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":70,\\\"end_line\\\":80,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"S44-client-reconnect\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"client/client.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":154,\\\"end_line\\\":172,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}]\nfb={\\\"ref_ids\\\":[\\\"S42-delay-propose-chan\\\",\\\"S43-client-seqnum\\\",\\\"S44-client-reconnect\\\",\\\"S22-replica-listener\\\",\\\"S40-client-command-allocation\\\"],\n \\\"answered\\\":\\\"The delay-ordering helper's semantics are now understood and its one dangerous premise is bounded. When a latency table is configured, client proposals are passed through a per-connection ordering structure that hands proposals to the replica's propose channel only in a contiguous run starting from CommandId zero (the traversal compares the last handled id with the next node's id minus one, starting from -1). A connection whose first proposal carries any other CommandId would therefore never be delivered: the proposal sits in the structure and the client waits. The premise does not arise in this snapshot: the client numbers its proposals from zero (seqnum initialised to -1 and incremented before use) and creates one connection per run, and although Reconnect exists in the client it has no call site anywhere in the repository, so no connection is ever reused with a continuing command-id sequence.\\\",\n \\\"remaining\\\":[\\\"the helper's behaviour if a future caller reconnects with a continuing command-id sequence, which would stall that client's proposals\\\",\n   \\\"whether a deployment in this snapshot ever passes -latency, which decides whether the ordering path is active at all\\\",\n   \\\"the reader-side reaction to a displaced stream (codec family) and the two paused candidates' resume conditions\\\"],\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"This is a bounded negative result rather than a defect: the mechanism that would block a client exists, but the only client in the snapshot respects the assumption it depends on. Recording it closes the 'delay table's scheduling effect' unread item at the level of the ordering semantics and names the single premise a future change would break.\\\",\n \\\"understanding\\\":\\\"updated\\\",\\\"question_updates\\\":{}}\nsub={\\\"action\\\":\\\"research\\\",\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Records the delay-ordering helper's mechanism and the premise it depends on, together with the two client facts that make the premise hold in this snapshot. Three ranges are registered; no map object changes and no requirement, premise or observation is affected.\\\",\n \\\"sources\\\":srcs,\\\"feedback\\\":fb}\njson.dump(sub,open('submission-delay-note.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('ok')\nPY\ncd .. && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run --submission submission-delay-note.json 2>&1 | head -8\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
